export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"civilcraft","addon":"lightdesign","name":"Interior lighting design","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"lightdesign-start","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: getting started","summary":"What the Interior lighting design add-on works out, how to switch it on, and how to go from a room outline and a photometry file to lux, uniformity and glare in a few steps.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["lighting","illuminance","lux","3d","catalogue","catalog","dialux","relux","photometry","ies","ldt","eulumdat","uniformity","ugr","glare","luminaire","workplane","light loss factor","en 12464","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-the-numbers-are","text":"What the numbers are","level":2},{"id":"your-luminaire-catalogues","text":"Your luminaire catalogues","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInterior lighting design lights one room at a time. You give it the room (an outline, a ceiling height and how reflective the surfaces are), the luminaires (a maker&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.ies\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ldt\u003C/code\u003E file, or one of the built-in generic types) and where they go. It returns the illuminance on the workplane (the table-top height), how even it is, an estimate of glare (UGR), the light on walls and ceiling, and the watts per square metre.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works in CadCraft, BimCraft and ModelCraft (and outdoors in CivilCraft). Switch it on in Settings, General, Add-ons. It is off until you do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a road or street, tick \u003Cem\u003ERoad lighting\u003C/em\u003E at the top of the panel: see \u003Cem\u003EInterior lighting design: road and street lighting\u003C/em\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoom.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft, select a closed shape (a rectangle or polyline) and press Read again; choose it from Rooms found. In BimCraft the list holds the rooms the walls enclose. In ModelCraft, select one floor face and press Read again: the room is that face. Or type the outline in millimetres. Set the ceiling height, the workplane height (0.8 m for a desk) and the reflectance of ceiling, walls and floor (a light office is about 0.7, 0.5, 0.2).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELuminaire.\u003C/strong\u003E Load the maker&#039;s file, or pick a generic type to try it. A generic type is a typical figure, not a maker&#039;s. To choose among many, build a catalogue (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayout.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cem\u003EPlan for a target\u003C/em\u003E finds the fewest luminaires, in an even grid, that reach the mean lux you type. \u003Cem\u003EEven grid\u003C/em\u003E and \u003Cem\u003ERows\u003C/em\u003E place them as you say.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERequirements.\u003C/strong\u003E Pick a kind of room to fill in typical targets, or type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECalculate.\u003C/strong\u003E You get the numbers, ticks for the requirements met, and a colour map. \u003Cem\u003EDraw in the drawing\u003C/em\u003E puts the luminaires and the lux values on layers LD-LUMINAIRE and LD-LUX (CadCraft). In BimCraft it places the luminaires in the room&#039;s level as light fixtures (one undo step; they appear in the device schedule) and writes the calculated lux as numbers in that level&#039;s plan view. In ModelCraft it puts the luminaires and the 3D lux surface in the model as coloured bodies (one undo step), so they show in the 3D view and export with the model; they are pictures of the result, not lights ModelCraft knows. \u003Cem\u003EReport\u003C/em\u003E gives a page you can print to PDF; \u003Cem\u003ECSV\u003C/em\u003E gives every grid point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-numbers-are\"\u003EWhat the numbers are\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELux are \u003Cstrong\u003Emaintained\u003C/strong\u003E: the light loss factor (0.8 by default; set it from the maintenance plan) is applied to everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe light from the luminaires is added to the light that bounces off the floor, walls and ceiling, which are treated as matt surfaces. Two independent calculations agree: a photon-tracing check of a 6 × 4 m room matches the panel to within 1.4 %.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWalls, ceiling and floor do not block light from each other, so an L-shaped or otherwise non-box room comes out a little too bright in its bays. Furniture, columns and partitions you list do block the direct light, windows give a daylight factor and the escape route gets an emergency check: see \u003Cem\u003EWindows, furniture and emergency lighting\u003C/em\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUGR\u003C/strong\u003E is an estimate from the CIE formula, from the luminaires&#039; own candela and luminous opening. Compare it with the maker&#039;s UGR table before you rely on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe kinds of room and their targets are \u003Cstrong\u003Etypical values typed from memory of EN 12464-1\u003C/strong\u003E, not checked against the edition you work to. Confirm them, or type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELines of equal lux (isolines)\u003C/strong\u003E are drawn over the map (tick box under the result; also in the report). They are cut from the same grid as the table, so a line labelled 200 lies where the calculation gives 200 lx, to the grid&#039;s spacing. The lines stop at furniture and columns you listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe 3D lux surface\u003C/strong\u003E under the map shows the same grid as a landscape: height and colour are the lux at each point. Drag it to turn it, or type the turn and tilt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-luminaire-catalogues\"\u003EYour luminaire catalogues\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMakers publish a photometry file for each product (EULUMDAT \u003Ccode\u003E.ldt\u003C/code\u003E or IES \u003Ccode\u003E.ies\u003C/code\u003E), usually on the product&#039;s page or in their download area. Save the ones you use in a folder, then under \u003Cem\u003EYour luminaire catalogues\u003C/em\u003E press \u003Cem\u003EAdd a folder\u003C/em\u003E (or \u003Cem\u003EAdd files\u003C/em\u003E). Each file becomes a row: its name, the maker it names, lumens and watts as the file gives them, lumens per watt, and the beam angle worked out from the candela table (where the light falls to half its peak, the mean of the C0 and C90 planes). Search by name or maker, sort by lumens, watts, beam or efficacy, set a lowest lumens or highest watts, and press \u003Cem\u003EUse\u003C/em\u003E on the one you want. Name a catalogue to start another one (for example one per maker or per project).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe files stay on this device, in the add-on&#039;s own storage, as you added them: nothing is uploaded. A catalogue too big for that storage (about 1.5 MB of files in all) is kept until the page closes, and the panel says so. CadCraft makes no catalogue of its own and copies no maker&#039;s data.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Interior lighting design lights one room at a time. You give it the room (an outline, a ceiling height and how reflective the surfaces are), the luminaires (a maker's .ies or .ldt file, or one of the built-in generic types) and where they go. It returns the illuminance on the workplane (the table-top height), how even it is, an estimate of glare (UGR), the light on walls and ceiling, and the watts per square metre. It works in CadCraft, BimCraft and ModelCraft (and outdoors in CivilCraft). Switch it on in Settings, General, Add-ons. It is off until you do. For a road or street, tick Road lighting at the top of the panel: see Interior lighting design: road and street lighting. Steps Room. In CadCraft, select a closed shape (a rectangle or polyline) and press Read again; choose it from Rooms found. In BimCraft the list holds the rooms the walls enclose. In ModelCraft, select one floor face and press Read again: the room is that face. Or type the outline in millimetres. Set the ceiling height, the workplane height (0.8 m for a desk) and the reflectance of ceiling, walls and floor (a light office is about 0.7, 0.5, 0.2). Luminaire. Load the maker's file, or pick a generic type to try it. A generic type is a typical figure, not a maker's. To choose among many, build a catalogue (below). Layout. Plan for a target finds the fewest luminaires, in an even grid, that reach the mean lux you type. Even grid and Rows place them as you say. Requirements. Pick a kind of room to fill in typical targets, or type your own. Calculate. You get the numbers, ticks for the requirements met, and a colour map. Draw in the drawing puts the luminaires and the lux values on layers LD-LUMINAIRE and LD-LUX (CadCraft). In BimCraft it places the luminaires in the room's level as light fixtures (one undo step; they appear in the device schedule) and writes the calculated lux as numbers in that level's plan view. In ModelCraft it puts the luminaires and the 3D lux surface in the model as coloured bodies (one undo step), so they show in the 3D view and export with the model; they are pictures of the result, not lights ModelCraft knows. Report gives a page you can print to PDF; CSV gives every grid point. What the numbers are Lux are maintained: the light loss factor (0.8 by default; set it from the maintenance plan) is applied to everything. The light from the luminaires is added to the light that bounces off the floor, walls and ceiling, which are treated as matt surfaces. Two independent calculations agree: a photon-tracing check of a 6 × 4 m room matches the panel to within 1.4 %. Walls, ceiling and floor do not block light from each other, so an L-shaped or otherwise non-box room comes out a little too bright in its bays. Furniture, columns and partitions you list do block the direct light, windows give a daylight factor and the escape route gets an emergency check: see Windows, furniture and emergency lighting. UGR is an estimate from the CIE formula, from the luminaires' own candela and luminous opening. Compare it with the maker's UGR table before you rely on it. The kinds of room and their targets are typical values typed from memory of EN 12464-1, not checked against the edition you work to. Confirm them, or type your own. Lines of equal lux (isolines) are drawn over the map (tick box under the result; also in the report). They are cut from the same grid as the table, so a line labelled 200 lies where the calculation gives 200 lx, to the grid's spacing. The lines stop at furniture and columns you listed. The 3D lux surface under the map shows the same grid as a landscape: height and colour are the lux at each point. Drag it to turn it, or type the turn and tilt. Your luminaire catalogues Makers publish a photometry file for each product (EULUMDAT .ldt or IES .ies), usually on the product's page or in their download area. Save the ones you use in a folder, then under Your luminaire catalogues press Add a folder (or Add files). Each file becomes a row: its name, the maker it names, lumens and watts as the file gives them, lumens per watt, and the beam angle worked out from the candela table (where the light falls to half its peak, the mean of the C0 and C90 planes). Search by name or maker, sort by lumens, watts, beam or efficacy, set a lowest lumens or highest watts, and press Use on the one you want. Name a catalogue to start another one (for example one per maker or per project). The files stay on this device, in the add-on's own storage, as you added them: nothing is uploaded. A catalogue too big for that storage (about 1.5 MB of files in all) is kept until the page closes, and the panel says so. CadCraft makes no catalogue of its own and copies no maker's data. This is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result."},{"slug":"lightdesign-glare","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: glare, uniformity and the light loss factor","summary":"How the add-on reports uniformity and glare, where its UGR figure comes from, and what the light loss factor does.","section":"reference","order":2,"keywords":["ugr","glare","uniformity","uo","light loss factor","maintenance factor","workplane","margin","grid","lux"],"headings":[{"id":"uniformity","text":"Uniformity","level":2},{"id":"glare-ugr","text":"Glare (UGR)","level":2},{"id":"light-loss-factor","text":"Light loss factor","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"uniformity\"\u003EUniformity\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUniformity is the lowest lux divided by the mean lux over the calculation grid on the workplane. The grid is spaced by the rule of EN 12464-1 (finer for a smaller room) and leaves out an edge strip (0.5 m by default) the way the standard does for the task area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"glare-ugr\"\u003EGlare (UGR)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUGR comes from the CIE 117 formula, UGR = 8 log10 (0.25 / Lb × Σ L² ω / p²). Lb is the background luminance, from the light bounced off the room. L and ω are the luminance and the size of each luminaire seen from the eye, from its candela table and luminous opening. p is the position index. Four observers sit at 1.2 m, in the middle of each side of the room, looking along the room; the worst of them is shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an estimate. It treats the luminous opening as a flat face and uses the full (initial) light output. The maker&#039;s UGR table, made the standard way, is the reference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"light-loss-factor\"\u003ELight loss factor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELamps lose output and luminaires collect dirt. The light loss factor (default 0.8) multiplies every lux figure, so the numbers show what the room gives when it needs cleaning, not when it is new. Set it from the maintenance plan.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Uniformity Uniformity is the lowest lux divided by the mean lux over the calculation grid on the workplane. The grid is spaced by the rule of EN 12464-1 (finer for a smaller room) and leaves out an edge strip (0.5 m by default) the way the standard does for the task area. Glare (UGR) UGR comes from the CIE 117 formula, UGR = 8 log10 (0.25 / Lb × Σ L² ω / p²). Lb is the background luminance, from the light bounced off the room. L and ω are the luminance and the size of each luminaire seen from the eye, from its candela table and luminous opening. p is the position index. Four observers sit at 1.2 m, in the middle of each side of the room, looking along the room; the worst of them is shown. It is an estimate. It treats the luminous opening as a flat face and uses the full (initial) light output. The maker's UGR table, made the standard way, is the reference. Light loss factor Lamps lose output and luminaires collect dirt. The light loss factor (default 0.8) multiplies every lux figure, so the numbers show what the room gives when it needs cleaning, not when it is new. Set it from the maintenance plan."},{"slug":"lightdesign-daylight","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: windows, furniture and emergency lighting","summary":"How to add windows for a daylight factor, boxes that block light, and an escape route for an emergency lighting check, and what each of them leaves out.","section":"reference","order":3,"keywords":["daylight","daylight factor","window","glazing","overcast sky","furniture","partition","obstruction","emergency lighting","escape route","en 1838","lux","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"windows-and-daylight","text":"Windows and daylight","level":2},{"id":"furniture-and-partitions","text":"Furniture and partitions","level":2},{"id":"emergency-lighting","text":"Emergency lighting","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"windows-and-daylight\"\u003EWindows and daylight\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cem\u003EWindows and daylight\u003C/em\u003E type one window per line: \u003Ccode\u003Ewall, from, width, sill, head, transmittance\u003C/code\u003E in millimetres. The wall is counted from the outline: 0 is the wall from corner 0 to corner 1, 1 the next, and so on. \u003Cem\u003EFrom\u003C/em\u003E is the distance along that wall to the window&#039;s left edge, \u003Cem\u003Esill\u003C/em\u003E and \u003Cem\u003Ehead\u003C/em\u003E are the heights of its bottom and top. The transmittance (how much light the glass lets through, 0.7 if you leave it out) is the real figure from the glass maker. In BimCraft, picking a room fills the list from the model&#039;s windows; the model does not give a window&#039;s height, so \u003Cstrong\u003E1.2 m is assumed\u003C/strong\u003E: correct it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is the \u003Cstrong\u003Edaylight factor\u003C/strong\u003E on the workplane: the light inside as a percentage of the light outside, under the standard overcast sky (the CIE sky, brightest at the zenith). It gives the mean, the lowest and highest, the uniformity, the glass area and, for the light outside you choose, the lux. Type a daylight factor and the panel says how much of the room reaches it. No required value is built in: use the one your rules ask for.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat it leaves out: no sun and no clear sky, no light reflected from the ground, no buildings, trees or overhangs outside, the same share of light passing the glass at every angle, no frames, bars or curtains. The wall behind the glass reflects nothing (the opening lets light out as well). Daylight is worked out alone; it is not added to the electric light.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow it was checked: with the sky law worked by hand, a window that sees the whole sky overhead gives the full outside illuminance, an endless vertical window gives exactly half of it on a horizontal plane, and a vertical plane facing the sky gets 0.396 of it; the light that enters a room through a window equals the light the sky puts on that window, to within 0.2 % on the test room. Nobody has compared a room with another program&#039;s result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"furniture-and-partitions\"\u003EFurniture and partitions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EList boxes that block the direct light, one per line: \u003Ccode\u003Ex0, y0, x1, y1, z0, z1\u003C/code\u003E in millimetres (a partition is a thin tall box). A luminaire reaches a point only if the straight line between them crosses no box. Light that has bounced off the walls, floor or ceiling is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E blocked, so behind a big box the result is a little too bright, never too dark. Points inside a box (a column) are left out of the figures.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"emergency-lighting\"\u003EEmergency lighting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cem\u003ECheck emergency lighting\u003C/em\u003E, say which luminaires are on the emergency circuit (every n-th one), what share of their rated light they give in an emergency, and draw the escape route as points. You get the lowest and highest lux on the route&#039;s centre line and the lowest lux over the open area, and ticks against three limits.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe limits (1 lx on the route, highest to lowest at most 40 to 1, 0.5 lx over the open area) are \u003Cstrong\u003Etypical values in the style of EN 1838, typed from memory and not checked against the edition you work to; they wait for a lighting engineer to confirm them.\u003C/strong\u003E Type your own. Only the direct light on the floor is counted. Signs, how long the batteries last and the switch-over time are not checked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIsolines and a 3D picture of the result.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoosing luminaires from a maker&#039;s catalogue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExterior and road lighting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDaylight from the sun, from clear skies, or with buildings outside; glare from windows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEmergency lighting signs, duration and light bounced off the room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Windows and daylight Under Windows and daylight type one window per line: wall, from, width, sill, head, transmittance in millimetres. The wall is counted from the outline: 0 is the wall from corner 0 to corner 1, 1 the next, and so on. From is the distance along that wall to the window's left edge, sill and head are the heights of its bottom and top. The transmittance (how much light the glass lets through, 0.7 if you leave it out) is the real figure from the glass maker. In BimCraft, picking a room fills the list from the model's windows; the model does not give a window's height, so 1.2 m is assumed: correct it. The result is the daylight factor on the workplane: the light inside as a percentage of the light outside, under the standard overcast sky (the CIE sky, brightest at the zenith). It gives the mean, the lowest and highest, the uniformity, the glass area and, for the light outside you choose, the lux. Type a daylight factor and the panel says how much of the room reaches it. No required value is built in: use the one your rules ask for. What it leaves out: no sun and no clear sky, no light reflected from the ground, no buildings, trees or overhangs outside, the same share of light passing the glass at every angle, no frames, bars or curtains. The wall behind the glass reflects nothing (the opening lets light out as well). Daylight is worked out alone; it is not added to the electric light. How it was checked: with the sky law worked by hand, a window that sees the whole sky overhead gives the full outside illuminance, an endless vertical window gives exactly half of it on a horizontal plane, and a vertical plane facing the sky gets 0.396 of it; the light that enters a room through a window equals the light the sky puts on that window, to within 0.2 % on the test room. Nobody has compared a room with another program's result. Furniture and partitions List boxes that block the direct light, one per line: x0, y0, x1, y1, z0, z1 in millimetres (a partition is a thin tall box). A luminaire reaches a point only if the straight line between them crosses no box. Light that has bounced off the walls, floor or ceiling is not blocked, so behind a big box the result is a little too bright, never too dark. Points inside a box (a column) are left out of the figures. Emergency lighting Tick Check emergency lighting, say which luminaires are on the emergency circuit (every n-th one), what share of their rated light they give in an emergency, and draw the escape route as points. You get the lowest and highest lux on the route's centre line and the lowest lux over the open area, and ticks against three limits. The limits (1 lx on the route, highest to lowest at most 40 to 1, 0.5 lx over the open area) are typical values in the style of EN 1838, typed from memory and not checked against the edition you work to; they wait for a lighting engineer to confirm them. Type your own. Only the direct light on the floor is counted. Signs, how long the batteries last and the switch-over time are not checked. Not built yet Isolines and a 3D picture of the result. Choosing luminaires from a maker's catalogue. Exterior and road lighting. Daylight from the sun, from clear skies, or with buildings outside; glare from windows. Emergency lighting signs, duration and light bounced off the room. This is a design aid, not a certificate: the person responsible for the lighting design checks the result."},{"slug":"lightdesign-night","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: site lighting and the night view","summary":"How to light an outdoor area, draw a night picture lit only by your luminaires, read the false-colour view beside it, and what the picture and numbers leave out.","section":"reference","order":4,"keywords":["night render","night view","site lighting","exterior lighting","car park","false colour","ies","ldt","colour temperature","lumens","luminaire","renderer","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"site-lighting","text":"Site lighting","level":2},{"id":"the-night-view","text":"The night view","level":2},{"id":"what-it-leaves-out","text":"What it leaves out","level":2},{"id":"the-model-s-own-surfaces-bimcraft","text":"The model's own surfaces (BimCraft)","level":2},{"id":"landscape-lights-civilcraft","text":"Landscape lights (CivilCraft)","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"site-lighting\"\u003ESite lighting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cem\u003EOutdoors: site lighting\u003C/em\u003E under \u003Cem\u003ERoom\u003C/em\u003E. The outline is now the area to light (a car park, a path, a forecourt), the ceiling height becomes the mounting height of the poles, and the workplane is the ground (0 mm). Pick a pole luminaire or a bollard, or load your own .ies or .ldt file, and place them as a grid or in rows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is the \u003Cstrong\u003Edirect\u003C/strong\u003E light of the luminaires on the ground: mean, lowest and highest lux and the uniformity, from the measured light spread of each luminaire. Boxes you list under \u003Cem\u003EFurniture and partitions\u003C/em\u003E (buildings, trees) shade it. There is no light bounced back from the ground or the buildings, and no plan-for-a-target mode outdoors: try a grid and read the numbers. The required lux for a car park or a road come from your rules; none is built in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-night-view\"\u003EThe night view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the result, \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw the night view\u003C/strong\u003E draws the area at night, lit only by your luminaires. Each luminaire becomes a lamp with its measured light spread (the .ies or .ldt file is read by CadCraft, and the picture uses the same light spread the calculation uses), its lumens (after the maintenance factor) and its colour temperature (the \u003Cem\u003EColour temperature\u003C/em\u003E box, 3000 K to start; a warm light is orange, a cold one blue). The exposure is set from the mean lux so the picture is neither black nor white.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBeside it is the \u003Cstrong\u003Efalse-colour view\u003C/strong\u003E: the same camera, with the workplane or the ground painted in lux, dark blue to red on a log scale, and a legend of round values. The colours come from the same calculation as the table, not from the picture, so a red patch is where the table&#039;s highest lux are. Where the picture and the numbers can be compared (a matt floor, one luminaire) they agree to within 5 %; in a room with bounced light, within about 5 % on the floor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn BimCraft the picture is drawn on this page by the renderer&#039;s reference path tracer (slow; Draft is quick). In CadCraft there is no renderer, so you get the false-colour view and a \u003Cem\u003ELamps for a render\u003C/em\u003E file (the lamps, their light spreads and the night settings as JSON) for a renderer to use. Drawing the luminaires into a BimCraft model also gives each fixture a \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Light\u003C/code\u003E property set, so BimCraft&#039;s own Render dialog lights the model with them (its Night preset puts the sun down).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-leaves-out\"\u003EWhat it leaves out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft, and for a site, the picture is the room or the site from your outline with plain grey surfaces, not your model, except the boxes you list. In BimCraft a room chosen from the model is drawn with the model&#039;s own surfaces (see below); the numbers still use the outline and its reflectances.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe renderer&#039;s matt surface also reflects a little specular light, so the picture can be about 10 % brighter than the numbers on a dark floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA light spread is carried at 64 by 32 angles: a beam narrower than about 3 degrees is smoothed. More than 32 different files in one design: the rest are drawn as plain lamps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELuminaires are small spheres of light; their body is not drawn, and there is no glare, light pollution or lighting for a road class.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENobody has compared a picture with another lighting program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-model-s-own-surfaces-bimcraft\"\u003EThe model&#039;s own surfaces (BimCraft)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a room from \u003Cem\u003ERooms found\u003C/em\u003E in BimCraft and draw the night view: the picture shows the model as it is round that room, its walls, floor, ceiling and what stands in it, in the colours of the 3D view (see-through colours as glass). Where the model has no floor or no ceiling over the room, the outline&#039;s own is used, and the line under the picture says so. One luminaire over the model&#039;s own ground was checked against the calculation to within 5 %.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw\u003C/strong\u003E the luminaires into the model, each fixture carries the name of its light spread and the spreads are kept in the file (on the project, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_LightIes\u003C/code\u003E). BimCraft&#039;s own \u003Cstrong\u003ERender\u003C/strong\u003E dialog then lights each fixture with its measured light spread, not as a plain lamp: the lamp list shows the spread&#039;s name and the IES button shows the file. A light spread you load by hand for another lamp is kept.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"landscape-lights-civilcraft\"\u003ELandscape lights (CivilCraft)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch the add-on on in CivilCraft (Settings, Add-ons). \u003Cem\u003ERooms found\u003C/em\u003E lists the project&#039;s parking lots, parcels, paving and planting beds as outdoor areas, each measured from its own corner in millimetres. In an area with trees or Landscape paths, the \u003Cstrong\u003ELandscape\u003C/strong\u003E layout puts an up-light at the foot of each tree, a bollard every 5 m along each path, and calculates the direct lux on the ground like any site. CivilCraft has no renderer: you get the false-colour view, the report, the CSV and the \u003Cem\u003ELamps for a render\u003C/em\u003E file. The luminaires are not drawn into the CivilCraft project.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Site lighting Tick Outdoors: site lighting under Room. The outline is now the area to light (a car park, a path, a forecourt), the ceiling height becomes the mounting height of the poles, and the workplane is the ground (0 mm). Pick a pole luminaire or a bollard, or load your own .ies or .ldt file, and place them as a grid or in rows. The result is the direct light of the luminaires on the ground: mean, lowest and highest lux and the uniformity, from the measured light spread of each luminaire. Boxes you list under Furniture and partitions (buildings, trees) shade it. There is no light bounced back from the ground or the buildings, and no plan-for-a-target mode outdoors: try a grid and read the numbers. The required lux for a car park or a road come from your rules; none is built in. The night view Under the result, Draw the night view draws the area at night, lit only by your luminaires. Each luminaire becomes a lamp with its measured light spread (the .ies or .ldt file is read by CadCraft, and the picture uses the same light spread the calculation uses), its lumens (after the maintenance factor) and its colour temperature (the Colour temperature box, 3000 K to start; a warm light is orange, a cold one blue). The exposure is set from the mean lux so the picture is neither black nor white. Beside it is the false-colour view: the same camera, with the workplane or the ground painted in lux, dark blue to red on a log scale, and a legend of round values. The colours come from the same calculation as the table, not from the picture, so a red patch is where the table's highest lux are. Where the picture and the numbers can be compared (a matt floor, one luminaire) they agree to within 5 %; in a room with bounced light, within about 5 % on the floor. In BimCraft the picture is drawn on this page by the renderer's reference path tracer (slow; Draft is quick). In CadCraft there is no renderer, so you get the false-colour view and a Lamps for a render file (the lamps, their light spreads and the night settings as JSON) for a renderer to use. Drawing the luminaires into a BimCraft model also gives each fixture a CadCraft_Light property set, so BimCraft's own Render dialog lights the model with them (its Night preset puts the sun down). What it leaves out In CadCraft, and for a site, the picture is the room or the site from your outline with plain grey surfaces, not your model, except the boxes you list. In BimCraft a room chosen from the model is drawn with the model's own surfaces (see below); the numbers still use the outline and its reflectances. The renderer's matt surface also reflects a little specular light, so the picture can be about 10 % brighter than the numbers on a dark floor. A light spread is carried at 64 by 32 angles: a beam narrower than about 3 degrees is smoothed. More than 32 different files in one design: the rest are drawn as plain lamps. Luminaires are small spheres of light; their body is not drawn, and there is no glare, light pollution or lighting for a road class. Nobody has compared a picture with another lighting program. The model's own surfaces (BimCraft) Choose a room from Rooms found in BimCraft and draw the night view: the picture shows the model as it is round that room, its walls, floor, ceiling and what stands in it, in the colours of the 3D view (see-through colours as glass). Where the model has no floor or no ceiling over the room, the outline's own is used, and the line under the picture says so. One luminaire over the model's own ground was checked against the calculation to within 5 %. When you Draw the luminaires into the model, each fixture carries the name of its light spread and the spreads are kept in the file (on the project, CadCraft_LightIes). BimCraft's own Render dialog then lights each fixture with its measured light spread, not as a plain lamp: the lamp list shows the spread's name and the IES button shows the file. A light spread you load by hand for another lamp is kept. Landscape lights (CivilCraft) Switch the add-on on in CivilCraft (Settings, Add-ons). Rooms found lists the project's parking lots, parcels, paving and planting beds as outdoor areas, each measured from its own corner in millimetres. In an area with trees or Landscape paths, the Landscape layout puts an up-light at the foot of each tree, a bollard every 5 m along each path, and calculates the direct lux on the ground like any site. CivilCraft has no renderer: you get the false-colour view, the report, the CSV and the Lamps for a render file. The luminaires are not drawn into the CivilCraft project."},{"slug":"lightdesign-road","scope":"addon:lightdesign","title":"Interior lighting design: road and street lighting","summary":"How to light a straight road or street, what the illuminance, luminance, uniformity, threshold increment and edge illumination ratio mean here, where each method and class value comes from, and what is left out.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["road lighting","street lighting","carriageway","footway","luminance","r-table","reflection table","threshold increment","TI","veiling luminance","uniformity","Uo","Ul","edge illumination ratio","surround ratio","lighting class","M classes","C classes","P classes","RP-8","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"start","text":"Start","level":2},{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":2},{"id":"where-the-methods-and-values-come-from","text":"Where the methods and values come from","level":2},{"id":"what-it-leaves-out","text":"What it leaves out","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"start\"\u003EStart\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cem\u003ERoad lighting\u003C/em\u003E at the top of the panel. The room is replaced by a road.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe road.\u003C/strong\u003E In CivilCraft pick a road from the project: its corridor assembly becomes the cross-section (lanes next to the centre line make the carriageway, a walk is a footway, shoulders, gutters, curbs and planting strips are verges). Anywhere else, or to change it, type the cross-section, one strip a line from the left edge: \u003Ccode\u003Efootway, 2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecarriageway, 7, 2\u003C/code\u003E (width in metres and the number of lanes; add \u003Ccode\u003Eone-way\u003C/code\u003E for a one-way carriageway), \u003Ccode\u003Everge, 1.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emedian, 2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecycleway, 2\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe luminaire.\u003C/strong\u003E A pole to try the add-on, or your own .ies or .ldt file, or one from your catalogues. The file is read with C 90° pointing out over the road and C 0° along it; if your file is the other way round, \u003Cem\u003ETurn the file\u003C/em\u003E by 90° or 180°.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELuminaires.\u003C/strong\u003E One side (left or right), both sides opposite, both sides staggered (the second row half a spacing along), or twin arms on the centre (on the middle of the median). Spacing, mounting height, overhang (how far the luminaire reaches over the carriageway from its edge; negative means set back behind it) and tilt (towards the road).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe grid.\u003C/strong\u003E Points along the field and across each lane or strip. Type the grid your standard asks for: the defaults (10 along, 3 across a lane) are ours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe road surface.\u003C/strong\u003E To get luminance, paste a reflection table (an r-table, values r × 10 000) from your standard&#039;s table or a measured surface: first line the tan γ values, then one line a β angle with its values. Without one you get illuminance only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELighting class.\u003C/strong\u003E Type the values from your standard&#039;s class table, or start from one of the cited tables below and check them. Then \u003Cem\u003ECalculate\u003C/em\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe calculation field is the road between two neighbouring luminaires of a row. For each carriageway:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIlluminance\u003C/strong\u003E (lux, maintained): average, lowest and highest on the carriageway; Uo = lowest / average; Eavg / Emin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELuminance\u003C/strong\u003E (cd/m², maintained, only with a reflection table): an observer stands in the middle of each lane, 60 m before the field, eyes 1.5 m up, looking along the lane. Average L, overall uniformity Uo = lowest / average and longitudinal uniformity Ul = lowest / highest along the line of points in the observer&#039;s lane. The lowest value over the observers is shown, as the conventions say.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThreshold increment\u003C/strong\u003E TI (%) = 65 Lv / L^0.8, from initial values (no maintenance factor). Lv is the veiling luminance, K Σ E / θ² (K = 10, or from the observer&#039;s age), at an eye 1.5 m up looking 1° down along each lane, moved in steps along the field; luminaires up to 500 m ahead count, those above a line 20° up (the car&#039;s roof) do not. The highest Lv is used. The veiling luminance ratio Lv / L is shown too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdge illumination ratio\u003C/strong\u003E (the surround ratio): the average illuminance on a strip one lane wide just outside each edge of the carriageway over the one just inside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFootways and cycleways\u003C/strong\u003E: average, lowest and highest illuminance and Uo on each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach criterion you entered is checked; one not met becomes a warning in the app&#039;s Problems panel (where the app has it), and one that needs the reflection table you did not give becomes a note. The plan shows the field, the illuminance in colour and the luminaires. \u003Cem\u003ESave as CSV\u003C/em\u003E writes every point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-methods-and-values-come-from\"\u003EWhere the methods and values come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe road lighting standards (EN 13201-2 and -3, ANSI/IES RP-8, the CIE reflection tables) are not read here. The methods and values come from documents published openly, cited in the code and in \u003Ccode\u003Edocs/refs/road-lighting.md\u003C/code\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EK. Sørensen, \u003Cem\u003ERoad lighting\u003C/em\u003E, textbook for the Nordic road administrations&#039; NMF, 2013: the illuminance and luminance equations (pp. 70-72), the observer, field and reflection-table conventions (pp. 73-79, 88-92), the veiling luminance, its constant and the threshold increment (pp. 94-95), the edge illumination ratio (p. 103), and the EN 13201-2 M, C and P class tables as it reproduces them (Tables 2-4, pp. 16-17).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ES. Fotios, P. Boyce and C. Ellis, \u003Cem\u003EThe effect of pavement material on road lighting performance\u003C/em\u003E, for the UK Department for Transport, 2005 (pp. 9-12): the reduced luminance coefficient and the r-table layout.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFHWA-HRT-14-051, \u003Cem\u003EDesign criteria for adaptive roadway lighting\u003C/em\u003E, US FHWA, 2014, Table 20: the ANSI/IES RP-8-00 illuminance criteria as reproduced there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA class picked from these tables fills the boxes; your standard&#039;s edition or your country may differ (EN 13201-2 itself lets a country change Ul, TI and the edge ratio). \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing here says a road meets a standard: check against your class table.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-leaves-out\"\u003EWhat it leaves out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo reflection table is shipped: luminance, Ul, TI and the veiling luminance ratio need the one you paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe grid rule of EN 13201-3 is not built in: the grid is what you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA straight, level road only: no curves, junctions, roundabouts, slopes, conflict areas of other shapes, or wet roads (wet Uo).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo semi-cylindrical, hemispherical or vertical illuminance, no HS, SC or EV classes, no glare classes for luminaires, no energy indicators (EN 13201-5).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDirect light only. The eye illuminance for glare is taken on a plane facing along the line of sight (our reading of the source).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft the road is read from its assembly; the luminaires are not drawn into the project. Nobody has compared a result with another road lighting program yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Start Tick Road lighting at the top of the panel. The room is replaced by a road. The road. In CivilCraft pick a road from the project: its corridor assembly becomes the cross-section (lanes next to the centre line make the carriageway, a walk is a footway, shoulders, gutters, curbs and planting strips are verges). Anywhere else, or to change it, type the cross-section, one strip a line from the left edge: footway, 2, carriageway, 7, 2 (width in metres and the number of lanes; add one-way for a one-way carriageway), verge, 1.5, median, 2, cycleway, 2. The luminaire. A pole to try the add-on, or your own .ies or .ldt file, or one from your catalogues. The file is read with C 90° pointing out over the road and C 0° along it; if your file is the other way round, Turn the file by 90° or 180°. Luminaires. One side (left or right), both sides opposite, both sides staggered (the second row half a spacing along), or twin arms on the centre (on the middle of the median). Spacing, mounting height, overhang (how far the luminaire reaches over the carriageway from its edge; negative means set back behind it) and tilt (towards the road). The grid. Points along the field and across each lane or strip. Type the grid your standard asks for: the defaults (10 along, 3 across a lane) are ours. The road surface. To get luminance, paste a reflection table (an r-table, values r × 10 000) from your standard's table or a measured surface: first line the tan γ values, then one line a β angle with its values. Without one you get illuminance only. Lighting class. Type the values from your standard's class table, or start from one of the cited tables below and check them. Then Calculate. What you get The calculation field is the road between two neighbouring luminaires of a row. For each carriageway: Illuminance (lux, maintained): average, lowest and highest on the carriageway; Uo = lowest / average; Eavg / Emin. Luminance (cd/m², maintained, only with a reflection table): an observer stands in the middle of each lane, 60 m before the field, eyes 1.5 m up, looking along the lane. Average L, overall uniformity Uo = lowest / average and longitudinal uniformity Ul = lowest / highest along the line of points in the observer's lane. The lowest value over the observers is shown, as the conventions say. Threshold increment TI (%) = 65 Lv / L^0.8, from initial values (no maintenance factor). Lv is the veiling luminance, K Σ E / θ² (K = 10, or from the observer's age), at an eye 1.5 m up looking 1° down along each lane, moved in steps along the field; luminaires up to 500 m ahead count, those above a line 20° up (the car's roof) do not. The highest Lv is used. The veiling luminance ratio Lv / L is shown too. Edge illumination ratio (the surround ratio): the average illuminance on a strip one lane wide just outside each edge of the carriageway over the one just inside. Footways and cycleways: average, lowest and highest illuminance and Uo on each. Each criterion you entered is checked; one not met becomes a warning in the app's Problems panel (where the app has it), and one that needs the reflection table you did not give becomes a note. The plan shows the field, the illuminance in colour and the luminaires. Save as CSV writes every point. Where the methods and values come from The road lighting standards (EN 13201-2 and -3, ANSI/IES RP-8, the CIE reflection tables) are not read here. The methods and values come from documents published openly, cited in the code and in docs/refs/road-lighting.md: K. Sørensen, Road lighting, textbook for the Nordic road administrations' NMF, 2013: the illuminance and luminance equations (pp. 70-72), the observer, field and reflection-table conventions (pp. 73-79, 88-92), the veiling luminance, its constant and the threshold increment (pp. 94-95), the edge illumination ratio (p. 103), and the EN 13201-2 M, C and P class tables as it reproduces them (Tables 2-4, pp. 16-17). S. Fotios, P. Boyce and C. Ellis, The effect of pavement material on road lighting performance, for the UK Department for Transport, 2005 (pp. 9-12): the reduced luminance coefficient and the r-table layout. FHWA-HRT-14-051, Design criteria for adaptive roadway lighting, US FHWA, 2014, Table 20: the ANSI/IES RP-8-00 illuminance criteria as reproduced there. A class picked from these tables fills the boxes; your standard's edition or your country may differ (EN 13201-2 itself lets a country change Ul, TI and the edge ratio). Nothing here says a road meets a standard: check against your class table. What it leaves out No reflection table is shipped: luminance, Ul, TI and the veiling luminance ratio need the one you paste. The grid rule of EN 13201-3 is not built in: the grid is what you type. A straight, level road only: no curves, junctions, roundabouts, slopes, conflict areas of other shapes, or wet roads (wet Uo). No semi-cylindrical, hemispherical or vertical illuminance, no HS, SC or EV classes, no glare classes for luminaires, no energy indicators (EN 13201-5). Direct light only. The eye illuminance for glare is taken on a plane facing along the line of sight (our reading of the source). In CivilCraft the road is read from its assembly; the luminaires are not drawn into the project. Nobody has compared a result with another road lighting program yet."}]};
